ttention? Not mentioned, but maybe good. Crew cooperation: grumpy, uncooperative - big negative. Communication: not mentioned but likely poor. Responsibility: not mentioned. Adaptability: not mentioned.\n\nCandidate A: reliability? Not directly, but can anticipate dangerous situations (good), leadership, but unorganized (bad), not good at criticism (bad). Stress? Not mentioned. Technical competence: sees complex connections, spatial vision (good). Decision quality? Not mentioned. Crew cooperation: leadership good but not good at criticism, maybe poor. Responsibility? Not mentioned.\n\nCandidate C: decision quality (quick correct decisions) good, stress? Not mentioned but maybe calm. Technical competence? Not mentioned but maybe. Crew cooperation: difficulty communicating, egocentric, not willing to further education (bad). Reliability: regarded as 100% reliable (good). So reliability strong, but cooperation poor.\n\nCandidate D: responsibility serious (good), concentration (good), problem solving (good), responds to unexpected events adequately (stress resilience decent). Crew cooperation: arrogant, weak leadership (bad). Reliability? Not mentioned. Technical competence? Not mentioned.\n\nThus each has trade-offs. Candidate C has reliability and decision quality, but communication issues. Candidate B has reliability and stress, but cooperation issues. Candidate A has anticipation and leadership, but organization and criticism. Candidate D has responsibility and concentration, but arrogance.\n\nWhich is strongest overall? Might be Candidate C because reliability and decision quality are crucial, and communication issues could be mitigated? But egocentric and not willing to further education are serious for long-distance flights. Candidate B's interpersonal issues also serious.\n\nMaybe we can argue that Candidate A's anticipation of dangerous situations is critical for safety, and leadership is valuable, and his weaknesses (unorganized, not good at criticism) are less severe than others' egocentric or arrogance. So we could shift recommendation to Candidate A.\n\nWe need to reference Agent 2's points and bring our own facts. Provide evidence: Candidate A anticipates dangerous situations (operational reliability, decision quality), sees complex connections (technical competence), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation, responsibility). Concerns: not good at taking criticism (crew cooperation), unorganized (reliability). Compare to B: B's stress and reliability but interpersonal issues. Argue that A's strengths outweigh B's concerns.\n\nThus we can change vote to Candidate A.\n\nWe must include metadata vote.\n\nNow produce public message: \"I appreciate Agent 2's recommendation of B, but I think A is stronger overall because ...\". Provide evidence and implication.\n\nProceed."}
